{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## PS4 — an in-repo consumer this change BROKE, and a correction to my own claim\n\nThe Gerrit `api-compat` criterion caught something I had missed: I audited external consumers\nand documented the migration, but never audited **rebar's own in-repo consumers** of CLI JSON.\nTwo existed, and this change broke both.\n\n### The consumers\nBoth parse `rebar show --output json` and type-checked the comment `timestamp` with\n`isinstance(ts, (int, float))`. A string fails that check, so the loop skips every marker\ncomment, the helper always returns `False`, and the 24h duplicate-suppression window stops\nmatching:\n\n1. `rebar_reconciler.conflict_bug_filing._recent_marker_comment` — the reconciler's conflict\n   bug filer (the one the bot named).\n2. `scripts/alert_dedup.recent_marker_comment` — shared by the `canary_bridge` and\n   `dependency_audit` alert lanes (a sibling the finding did **not** name).\n\nBoth are deliberately **fail-soft**, so the break was silent: no exception, no bad exit — just\nan accumulation comment appended on every cycle to a ticket the lane had already filed. That is\nthe same silent-degradation failure mode this whole ticket exists to eliminate, which makes it a\nfitting last catch. Both now coerce with `int()` first.\n\n**Blast radius bounded by sweep, not assumption.** I enumerated every `json.loads` of rebar CLI\nstdout across `src/` and `scripts/`. These two were the only affected consumers; the rest parse\nJira `acli`, pyright, ripgrep, the GitHub Actions API, or OSV. I also re-verified the library\nfinding rather than restating it: `_lib_reads._json_or` handles quality-gate subprocess output\nand `_lib_ops` parses a reconcile plan — neither carries ns timestamps — and library ticket\nreads remain in-process Python objects with no JSON boundary.\n\n### Correction to my earlier claim\nI stated in a previous comment that \"no schema change was needed\". That was true of the field\n**types** but **false** of one description: `sign_result.schema.json`'s top-level description\nasserted that `signed_at` is \"carried as an INTEGER on CLI/library output and as a decimal\nSTRING over MCP\" — which this change makes false. It now states the single cross-surface rule,\nand `docs/release-notes.md` / `CHANGELOG.md` are corrected to say \"no field was RETYPED\" rather\nthan \"no schema change was needed\".\n\n### Two things needing human attention\n1. **`file_impact` is now incomplete and I deliberately did not update it.** The change touches\n   `scripts/alert_dedup.py`, `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/conflict_bug_filing.py`,\n   `src/rebar/schemas/sign_result.schema.json`, `docs/output-schemas.md` and two more test files\n   beyond what is recorded. Widening the recorded `file_impact` would stale the plan-review\n   attestation (its material pins `file_impact 270d8587e151dc32 34`) and re-trigger\n   `bugfix-size-attestation` on the open change. Reconcile after landing.\n2. **Provenance:** the `scripts/alert_dedup.py` hunk and its test appeared in this worktree from\n   a **concurrent session on this host** — I did not author them. I am carrying them because they\n   fix a real consumer this change would otherwise break, and I verified them independently\n   (RED with the coercion reverted, GREEN with it, liveness case present) rather than on trust.\n   Disclosed on the Gerrit change so authorship can be attributed before submit."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787960850872099107,"uuid":"3af0c71b-e5c4-410c-b2fc-d5b37935f818"}